## Bubble Sort

## Approach
-- Divide the array into sorted + unsorted parts.
-- Compare adjacent elements which is arr[j] with arr[j+1].
-- If arr[j] > arr[j+1], swap them.
-- After each pass, the largest element goes to the end.
-- Therefore, after each pass, we reduce the j condition range using n - i - 1.

## Complexity 
Time Complexity =:  
Best Case = O(n) -> array is sorted
Worst and Avg Case = O(n²)
Space Complexity = O(1)

SOLUTION :
static int[] BubbleSort(int[] arr, int n) {
        for (int i = 0; i <= n-1; i++) {
                int swapped = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                    int temp  = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                    arr[j+1] = temp;
                    swapped = 1;
                }
            }
            // Array is sorted
            if (swapped == 0) {
                break;
            }
        }
        return arr;
    }
